    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d145">

                <pb facs="0532" n="518" sameAs="frus:frus1872p2v1/pg518"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Cushing,
                        Attorney-General, to Mr. McKeon, district
                        attorney.</hi>
                </head>
                <dateline><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Attorney-General’s
                        Office</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1855-04-25"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">April</hi> 25, 1855</date>.</dateline>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Secretary of State having referred me
                    to your letter of the 24th instant, with its inclosures, I have taken the
                    directions of the President on the subject, and in accordance therewith have to
                    request you to institute legal proceedings against *Colonel Kinney and the
                    steamer United States, provided, in, your judgment, the evidence accessible will
                    suffice to afford probable cause. <seg type="margin">[179]</seg></p>
                <p>I have the honor to be, &amp;c., &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>C. CUSHING.</signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">John McKeon</hi>, Esq.,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">United States District Attorney, New York.</hi></p>
                    <p>P. S.—Since writing the above, I have seen the letter of Mr. Fabens in the
                        New York Herald of yesterday, which seems to require that he be proceeded
                        against equally with Colonel Kenney.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <signed>C. C.</signed>
                    </closer>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
